WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.329 --> 00:00:03.869
Cells are the champion escape artists,

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:07.049
able to squirm through narrow passages less than

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.800
one-quarter of their width.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.000
Two new studies show that cells

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.160
don’t emerge unscathed from these feats of contortion.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.060
A tight squeeze can burst the membrane

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.600
that surrounds the cell’s nucleus and damage its DNA.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.930
Here, several cancer cells are slithering through

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:26.709
a miniature obstacle course.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.420
When a cell has to struggle through a narrow passageway,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.220
its nucleus pops, spilling the green marker into its body.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.680
A leaky nucleus could be disastrous for a cell.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.120
But the studies also show that

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.540
cells quickly repair the ruptures, allowing

00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:45.459
them to return the green molecule to the nucleus

00:00:45.459 --> 00:00:47.899
and prepare for the next tight squeeze.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.720
This finding could open the way for a cancer treatment.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.410
If this repair process could be blocked with a drug,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.060
cancer cells might squeeze themselves to death.

